WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.209 --> 00:00:09.256
I want you all to imagine a place
where female athletes could come

00:00:09.256 --> 00:00:12.676
and they could get
interdisciplinary medical care

00:00:12.676 --> 00:00:15.554
and they could get the answers
to their questions

00:00:15.595 --> 00:00:18.098
so that they could achieve
peak performance

00:00:18.139 --> 00:00:19.849
throughout their life span.

00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:21.726
Where they could get educated

00:00:21.768 --> 00:00:24.896
about the physical changes
that happen during puberty,

00:00:24.938 --> 00:00:27.732
where they could learn
how to use their menstrual cycle

00:00:27.732 --> 00:00:29.734
to their performance advantage,

00:00:29.776 --> 00:00:33.613
where they could get sex-specific
injury prevention advice,

00:00:33.655 --> 00:00:35.574
where they could get nutritional advice,

00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:37.534
mental health support,

00:00:37.576 --> 00:00:41.413
where they could learn how to train
through pregnancy and postpartum,

00:00:41.454 --> 00:00:43.164
where they could learn how to train

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:45.292
through the menopausal
transition and beyond.

00:00:45.792 --> 00:00:48.461
We know that female sports involvement

00:00:48.503 --> 00:00:50.547
correlates with female success.

00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:54.092
So isn't it time that we started
investing in our female athletes

00:00:54.092 --> 00:00:55.635
and giving what they need

00:00:55.677 --> 00:00:58.805
so that they can actually
get to their full potential?

00:01:00.974 --> 00:01:03.018
So I was born in 1972.

00:01:03.018 --> 00:01:05.520
(Cheering)

00:01:05.562 --> 00:01:07.606
That was a banner year, as we know,

00:01:07.647 --> 00:01:10.025
because that was the year of Title IX.

00:01:10.066 --> 00:01:14.195
And Title IX was this
unbelievable legislation

00:01:14.237 --> 00:01:17.032
that required that basically,

00:01:17.032 --> 00:01:19.993
there were equal sporting
opportunities for girls and women

00:01:19.993 --> 00:01:22.120
in federally funded programs.

00:01:22.746 --> 00:01:27.584
So it's not really surprising that I would
grow up to be an elite athlete,

00:01:27.626 --> 00:01:31.546
a sports medicine doctor,
and a fierce advocate for women, right?

00:01:31.588 --> 00:01:32.714
I mean, check me out.

00:01:32.756 --> 00:01:35.592
Here I am crushing it
in a three-legged sack race.

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:37.260
(Laughter)

00:01:37.302 --> 00:01:39.304
And I started very young doing surgery.

00:01:39.304 --> 00:01:41.348
This is me operating on my brother,

00:01:41.389 --> 00:01:44.267
using a needle nose pliers
to try to remove his appendix.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:46.186
(Laughter)

00:01:46.186 --> 00:01:49.606
Actually, I wasn't that sporty
when I was a kid.

00:01:50.523 --> 00:01:53.526
I was much more into music and theater,

00:01:53.568 --> 00:01:56.363
big hair, big makeup, jazz hands.

00:01:56.404 --> 00:01:57.656
(Laughter)

00:01:57.697 --> 00:02:00.450
In fact, I didn't even pass

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.952
the president's physical fitness
test in sixth grade.

00:02:02.952 --> 00:02:04.287
(Laughter)

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:06.581
I was too slow at the 50-yard dash.

00:02:06.623 --> 00:02:08.708
Poor Mrs. Leonard,
she really tried with me.

00:02:09.542 --> 00:02:11.878
So no one was more surprised than I was

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.714
when I got to college
and I discovered rowing.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.009
They just let me walk on.

00:02:18.593 --> 00:02:21.513
There were no cuts, and I was not good.

00:02:22.639 --> 00:02:26.393
But I loved it and I was determined
to figure it out.

00:02:27.185 --> 00:02:30.689
And that's where my journey began
as a female athlete,

00:02:30.689 --> 00:02:34.943
where I learned so many of those lessons
that we all learn from sport:

00:02:34.943 --> 00:02:37.987
teamwork, perseverance, grit.

00:02:38.863 --> 00:02:41.032
I admit, I was a late bloomer,

00:02:41.074 --> 00:02:46.413
but I still reap so many of those benefits
that girls get from athletic involvement.

00:02:46.413 --> 00:02:48.331
I had improved fitness,

00:02:48.331 --> 00:02:51.209
overall better health, more confidence.

00:02:52.419 --> 00:02:56.965
We know that studies show that sports
participation in girls in high school

00:02:57.006 --> 00:02:58.550
and women in college

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:02.095
correlates with better GPAs
and higher graduation rates.

00:03:03.096 --> 00:03:04.639
I lived that statistic.

00:03:04.681 --> 00:03:06.891
I got good grades and I found my people.

00:03:07.392 --> 00:03:09.602
And eventually I even made the varsity.

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:13.690
And so by the time I graduated,
I was hungry for more.

00:03:13.732 --> 00:03:16.192
I wanted to see what my potential was.

00:03:17.485 --> 00:03:21.698
So I kept rowing, lots of rowing,
lots of studying,

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.826
and I even made the US national team.

00:03:25.535 --> 00:03:28.413
I did that while I was at Johns
Hopkins Medical School.

00:03:28.413 --> 00:03:30.457
I don't really recommend that combination.

00:03:30.457 --> 00:03:31.666
(Laughter)

00:03:31.666 --> 00:03:34.085
But my sixth grade gym teacher
was really proud.

00:03:34.127 --> 00:03:35.253
(Laughter)

00:03:35.754 --> 00:03:36.921
And I kept training.

00:03:36.963 --> 00:03:39.758
I did want to go to the Olympics,
but I wasn't fast enough.

00:03:39.758 --> 00:03:41.968
There were only two spots
for lightweight women

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:43.344
in the lightweight double.

00:03:43.344 --> 00:03:46.097
And so I had to head home in 2000.

00:03:46.097 --> 00:03:48.099
I went back to medical school,

00:03:48.141 --> 00:03:53.855
and I watched my friends row in Sydney
from patient rooms in the hospital,

00:03:53.855 --> 00:03:55.023
on the TV.

00:03:55.565 --> 00:03:57.400
But I really needed to shift my focus.

00:03:57.442 --> 00:03:59.694
I still loved sports,
and I had to figure out

00:03:59.736 --> 00:04:03.114
how I was going to combine
my medical career with women's sports.

00:04:04.115 --> 00:04:06.785
And I had learned a lot

00:04:06.785 --> 00:04:08.995
from my time at the Olympic
Training Center.

00:04:09.913 --> 00:04:13.666
We really don't know if I ever reached
my potential as an athlete,

00:04:13.708 --> 00:04:16.002
but that experience was so good for me.

00:04:16.377 --> 00:04:20.006
I experienced a lot, I witnessed a lot.

00:04:20.048 --> 00:04:21.549
We had a male coach

00:04:21.591 --> 00:04:24.719
who used to make us do a lot
of the same workouts as the men.

00:04:24.761 --> 00:04:28.056
It didn't matter whether we were
lightweights or we were open weights,

00:04:28.056 --> 00:04:30.099
whether we were in a single or an eight.

00:04:30.809 --> 00:04:32.769
We did do lactate testing,

00:04:32.811 --> 00:04:35.647
but we didn't get our blood checked
for iron deficiency

00:04:35.688 --> 00:04:37.607
or other nutritional deficiencies.

00:04:38.066 --> 00:04:40.151
We had body composition testing,

00:04:40.193 --> 00:04:42.612
but that was more for our coach to post

00:04:42.612 --> 00:04:45.323
and show us where we each
could lose more fat.

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:48.701
A lot of the lightweights lost
their menstrual cycle.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.412
We just thought that meant
we were training hard

00:04:51.454 --> 00:04:54.415
and we were working really hard,
and we we're nice and lean.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.586
You know, a lot of us developed
poor body image.

00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:03.716
And at that time, most of us didn't know
what the female athlete triad was.

00:05:03.716 --> 00:05:06.636
This combination
of low energy availability,

00:05:06.678 --> 00:05:09.931
where you don't eat enough calories
for how much you're expending,

00:05:09.973 --> 00:05:11.724
menstrual dysfunction

00:05:11.766 --> 00:05:14.561
and poor bone health and stress fractures.

00:05:15.311 --> 00:05:17.021
We had a doctor that would come in,

00:05:17.063 --> 00:05:19.774
it was usually a different doctor
every couple of weeks.

00:05:20.191 --> 00:05:23.945
Nobody asked us about our periods,
and we definitely didn’t talk about it.

00:05:25.321 --> 00:05:27.699
You know, even the open-weight
women had issues

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:32.120
where they would get urinary incontinence
if they tried to do the track workouts.

00:05:32.161 --> 00:05:34.747
So they were always trying
to dodge the track workouts.

00:05:34.747 --> 00:05:37.792
They'd come up with excuses
so they could skip those practices.

00:05:38.251 --> 00:05:41.212
And we didn't even know what normal
medical care should be.

00:05:41.504 --> 00:05:44.632
In rowing, a lot of us experienced
rib stress injuries.

00:05:44.674 --> 00:05:49.387
So those happen from overtraining,
underfueling or a combination.

00:05:49.888 --> 00:05:52.932
And so we didn't know
if it was appropriate or not

00:05:52.974 --> 00:05:55.602
when we went to see the doctor
about a rib stress injury

00:05:55.643 --> 00:05:56.895
that was off to the side

00:05:56.895 --> 00:05:59.188
and he was doing
a breast exam on all of us.

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:00.982
He didn't explain what he was doing.

00:06:00.982 --> 00:06:03.276
We just didn't really feel
comfortable with that.

00:06:03.276 --> 00:06:05.320
He obviously wasn't familiar with rowers.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.989
So I loved training with the other women,

00:06:07.989 --> 00:06:09.949
but I didn’t really like the environment.

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:12.327
And I thought that female
athletes deserved more.

00:06:13.286 --> 00:06:16.039
So when I headed back to med school,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.292
I decided to contact
the US Olympic Committee,

00:06:19.334 --> 00:06:22.420
what's now known as the US Olympic
and Paralympic Committee.

00:06:22.712 --> 00:06:25.131
And I thought, wow, it would be so great

00:06:25.131 --> 00:06:27.717
to talk to the women
who've been through all of this.

00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:28.927
Do they have a database

00:06:28.927 --> 00:06:33.681
of the former female athletes
that have been involved in Olympic sports?

00:06:33.681 --> 00:06:35.099
They said, "Of course we do.

00:06:35.099 --> 00:06:39.228
We have contact information
for over 700 people, 700 women."

00:06:39.228 --> 00:06:40.438
I thought, awesome.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.398
I would really like to survey them.

00:06:42.398 --> 00:06:45.443
I'd like to ask them
about their menstrual histories,

00:06:45.443 --> 00:06:46.903
about their fertility,

00:06:46.945 --> 00:06:49.614
about the different injuries
they might have had.

00:06:49.656 --> 00:06:51.658
How did they transition out of sport?

00:06:51.658 --> 00:06:53.785
What kind of exercise were they doing now?

00:06:53.826 --> 00:06:56.412
What were their long-term health issues?

00:06:56.412 --> 00:06:58.373
And you know what
the USOC said to me?

00:06:59.123 --> 00:07:00.291
"Hell, no."

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:03.378
"Why? This is a really good
group of people.

00:07:03.419 --> 00:07:05.296
What a great group to learn from."

00:07:05.797 --> 00:07:07.757
"Because we don't want to know.

00:07:07.757 --> 00:07:09.509
It'll look bad for women.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:11.511
And what are we going to do about it?"

00:07:12.095 --> 00:07:13.930
So I did what most women do.

00:07:14.347 --> 00:07:17.266
I felt bad and thought it was my fault.

00:07:17.308 --> 00:07:19.477
"Well, shoot, I didn't become an Olympian.

00:07:19.519 --> 00:07:21.854
So that's why they don't want
to share it with me"

00:07:21.896 --> 00:07:23.731
or, "I'm not done with med school yet.

00:07:23.731 --> 00:07:26.317
That must be why they're not
sharing this information."

00:07:26.359 --> 00:07:28.695
So then I did the other thing
that women often do.

00:07:28.736 --> 00:07:31.030
I decided to put my head down

00:07:31.072 --> 00:07:34.075
and just get overeducated
and overcompensate.

00:07:34.117 --> 00:07:37.245
So I finished med school.

00:07:37.704 --> 00:07:40.456
I went off and studied
biostatistics and epidemiology

00:07:40.456 --> 00:07:42.333
at the Harvard School of Public Health.

00:07:42.375 --> 00:07:45.461
I went to Penn and did my internal
medicine residency.

00:07:45.461 --> 00:07:48.381
And then I went back to Harvard
and did a sports fellowship

00:07:48.423 --> 00:07:50.091
and an endocrine fellowship.

00:07:50.758 --> 00:07:53.803
At this point, my family, my friends,

00:07:53.845 --> 00:07:55.596
my colleagues thought I was nuts.

00:07:56.222 --> 00:07:58.433
They were like,
"Will you just get on with it?

00:07:58.474 --> 00:08:01.602
Why are you doing all this schooling?
What are you afraid of?

00:08:01.644 --> 00:08:04.063
And if you're really interested
in sports medicine,

00:08:04.063 --> 00:08:06.190
why don't you become
an orthopedic surgeon?"

00:08:06.232 --> 00:08:09.402
Usually sports is just run
by orthopedic surgeons.

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:12.321
Well, I liked the OR,

00:08:12.321 --> 00:08:15.908
and I liked watching surgeons
put bones back together

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:18.202
and repair and reconstruct joints.

00:08:18.494 --> 00:08:21.122
But I wanted to do something
different with my career.

00:08:21.664 --> 00:08:24.083
I was more interested in physiology.

00:08:24.083 --> 00:08:27.211
I really thought that sports medicine
should be interdisciplinary.

00:08:27.253 --> 00:08:31.924
And I cared about endocrine, hormones
and cardiovascular systems.

00:08:31.924 --> 00:08:34.552
I cared about mental health
and nutrition.

00:08:35.636 --> 00:08:36.763
So then ...

00:08:37.305 --> 00:08:41.601
I was lucky to have a male boss
who actually believed in me.

00:08:41.642 --> 00:08:43.352
He was a he-for-she,

00:08:43.394 --> 00:08:46.522
an orthopedic surgeon
who understood what I was saying.

00:08:46.939 --> 00:08:52.236
And in 2013, he gave me the opportunity
to launch a female athlete program.

00:08:54.072 --> 00:08:57.033
That same year,

00:08:57.033 --> 00:08:59.327
my friends and I thought
it would be really cool

00:08:59.368 --> 00:09:01.287
to throw a female athlete conference.

00:09:01.287 --> 00:09:02.705
Now, we were scrappy,

00:09:02.705 --> 00:09:06.667
but we just wanted
to get coaches, athletes, parents,

00:09:06.667 --> 00:09:09.087
other medical professionals, all together

00:09:09.087 --> 00:09:13.007
so that we could talk about what we need
to know about female athletes.

00:09:13.341 --> 00:09:15.676
If people knew better,
they could do better.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:18.012
So we wanted to educate people.

00:09:18.054 --> 00:09:20.765
We wanted to get the network together.

00:09:20.765 --> 00:09:22.809
We wanted to collaborate.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:25.186
So there were international
people coming together

00:09:25.228 --> 00:09:27.021
and we were combining resources.

00:09:27.063 --> 00:09:30.108
And we wanted to inspire
other people to do this work.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:35.446
Our first conference was at a small
school outside of Boston.

00:09:35.446 --> 00:09:37.490
They donated the space to us.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:40.034
Our out-of-town speakers
stayed at my house.

00:09:40.368 --> 00:09:41.744
But over time, we've grown.

00:09:41.744 --> 00:09:44.997
It's now an international biennial
female athlete conference.

00:09:45.414 --> 00:09:48.292
The last one had people
from over 32 countries.

00:09:48.292 --> 00:09:50.962
And it’s something we really
look forward to every year.

00:09:51.003 --> 00:09:54.173
We’ve made huge connections,
and we’re doing more research together

00:09:54.173 --> 00:09:55.591
because of these connections.

00:09:56.342 --> 00:09:58.386
But there's so much more to do.

00:09:59.011 --> 00:10:03.683
We know that female athletes lag behind

00:10:03.683 --> 00:10:06.227
in terms of their participation in sport

00:10:06.269 --> 00:10:07.603
compared to boys.

00:10:07.937 --> 00:10:12.483
And this dropout rate really widens
as girls go through adolescence.

00:10:13.359 --> 00:10:15.153
So we need to be doing more

00:10:15.153 --> 00:10:19.574
because we know that sports actually
doesn't only help with physical health

00:10:19.615 --> 00:10:20.783
and mental health,

00:10:20.825 --> 00:10:24.704
but it also helps propel girls
into successful careers.

00:10:25.288 --> 00:10:30.168
80 percent of female
Fortune 500 executives were athletes.

00:10:30.918 --> 00:10:34.589
94 percent of the women
in C-suites did sports.

00:10:35.965 --> 00:10:39.177
But between 2014 and 2020,

00:10:39.177 --> 00:10:42.763
when you look at the top sports
exercise science journals,

00:10:42.763 --> 00:10:45.808
only six percent of the publications,

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:49.103
six percent of the articles,
focused solely on women.

00:10:49.937 --> 00:10:51.147
We've got to do better.

00:10:51.189 --> 00:10:55.693
We can't just apply studies
and data from male rats

00:10:55.735 --> 00:10:58.613
and male humans to girls and women.

00:10:58.654 --> 00:10:59.906
We're different.

00:11:01.949 --> 00:11:03.743
So we kept plugging away,

00:11:03.743 --> 00:11:05.119
and then 2020 hit.

00:11:05.745 --> 00:11:08.372
And the world as we know it
came to a screeching halt.

00:11:08.748 --> 00:11:13.169
We were taking care of our family,
our friends, our patients.

00:11:13.502 --> 00:11:15.171
We lost loved ones.

00:11:15.755 --> 00:11:20.218
We became depressed and stressed,
and our athletes did too.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:22.470
School was canceled.

00:11:22.511 --> 00:11:24.055
Sports were canceled.

00:11:24.096 --> 00:11:25.932
The Olympics were postponed.

00:11:26.807 --> 00:11:30.186
And yet our female athlete clinic exploded

00:11:30.228 --> 00:11:33.147
because our girls were handling the stress

00:11:33.189 --> 00:11:38.069
by overexercising, underfueling,
worrying about their body image.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.779
They were getting injuries,

00:11:39.779 --> 00:11:42.281
and they were getting
severe eating disorders.

00:11:43.908 --> 00:11:46.285
But two great things happened.

00:11:47.328 --> 00:11:50.581
One, in a full-circle moment,

00:11:50.623 --> 00:11:55.628
the new and improved US Olympic
and Paralympic Committee contacted me

00:11:55.628 --> 00:11:57.713
and asked me if I would cochair

00:11:57.755 --> 00:12:00.591
a newly-formed Women's Health Task force.

00:12:00.633 --> 00:12:01.759
What?

00:12:01.801 --> 00:12:02.969
Of course.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.972
(Applause and cheers)

00:12:06.806 --> 00:12:09.183
They wanted me to determine
the research needs

00:12:09.225 --> 00:12:12.019
and develop treatment protocols
for their athletes.

00:12:12.061 --> 00:12:13.312
This was a new era.

00:12:13.312 --> 00:12:16.107
Not only was I getting access
to the database,

00:12:16.148 --> 00:12:19.068
but they wanted transparency,
and they wanted to work together

00:12:19.068 --> 00:12:20.903
to help solve the issues.

00:12:22.029 --> 00:12:24.365
And the other cool thing that happened

00:12:24.407 --> 00:12:28.577
was that I was contacted
by the advisor to a philanthropist.

00:12:29.036 --> 00:12:30.663
Now, this is new territory for me.

00:12:30.705 --> 00:12:32.832
I don’t usually get these
kind of cold calls.

00:12:32.832 --> 00:12:35.960
But they wanted me to work
with five other individuals,

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:37.920
leaders from other institutions,

00:12:37.962 --> 00:12:42.300
to help design and launch
the Wu Tsai Human Performance Alliance.

00:12:43.092 --> 00:12:44.593
They wanted us to work together

00:12:44.593 --> 00:12:47.346
to discover principles
of peak performance,

00:12:47.388 --> 00:12:50.641
translate those
to youth athlete development,

00:12:50.641 --> 00:12:52.435
athletic excellence,

00:12:52.476 --> 00:12:56.605
resiliency and healthy, long life spans.

00:12:57.231 --> 00:13:01.193
And they wanted me to lead
the female athlete component of that.

00:13:01.944 --> 00:13:04.071
OK, So that should be
the end of my TED Talk,

00:13:04.071 --> 00:13:06.073
woo, and the rest of the world is great.

00:13:06.407 --> 00:13:08.326
But this propelled us,

00:13:08.367 --> 00:13:09.702
this got us to keep moving.

00:13:09.744 --> 00:13:12.079
We were already doing
all this international work,

00:13:12.079 --> 00:13:15.458
and this gave us more energy
and sped up the whole process.

00:13:15.791 --> 00:13:18.544
We were already working
on sex and menstrual cycle effects

00:13:18.586 --> 00:13:19.795
on performance,

00:13:19.795 --> 00:13:22.048
there's so much more
we need to learn there.

00:13:22.048 --> 00:13:24.967
We are working on relative energy
deficiency in sport.

00:13:25.009 --> 00:13:29.180
We continue our work with learning
about pregnancy and postpartum,

00:13:29.221 --> 00:13:31.766
psychological resilience,

00:13:31.807 --> 00:13:34.268
injury prevention and recovery,

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:40.024
the intersections of race, gender,
sexual orientation, class and ability,

00:13:40.066 --> 00:13:43.319
as well as the effects of menopause.

00:13:43.319 --> 00:13:44.862
I just told you I'm pushing 50.

00:13:44.904 --> 00:13:47.281
My teammates and I,
we want to know what's coming.

00:13:47.281 --> 00:13:48.449
(Laughter)

00:13:50.242 --> 00:13:52.411
But this is where you all come in.

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:55.956
Our sports systems, our medical systems,

00:13:55.998 --> 00:13:58.876
our cultures are not built
for girls and women.

00:14:00.211 --> 00:14:02.088
We don't have a one-stop shop,

00:14:02.088 --> 00:14:04.632
a physical facility to get
all of this work done.

00:14:04.673 --> 00:14:07.385
And people are looking to us,
the Female Athlete Program,

00:14:07.426 --> 00:14:09.095
to build this center.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:13.182
We really are the ones who are supposed
to be taking this deep dive.

00:14:13.224 --> 00:14:17.019
But we need to be working together,
and we need to have it all under one roof.

00:14:17.019 --> 00:14:18.729
Like the vision we talked about.

00:14:18.771 --> 00:14:20.439
Having the athletes come to us

00:14:20.481 --> 00:14:22.858
and have all this stuff
available in one place

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:25.694
and have sports scientists
working together with them.

00:14:26.654 --> 00:14:30.032
We want to do this
so that we can be a model.

00:14:30.074 --> 00:14:32.535
We're further along
than anywhere else in the world,

00:14:32.535 --> 00:14:35.037
but they're looking to us
so that we can be the model

00:14:35.079 --> 00:14:36.622
to help them build it elsewhere.

00:14:36.664 --> 00:14:39.125
And we can collaborate
and help let it grow.

00:14:40.501 --> 00:14:42.420
We've been trying to launch this vision,

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:47.007
but we cannot just rely on Clara Wu
and Joseph Tsai's generosity.

00:14:47.633 --> 00:14:50.428
We need to demand more from others.

00:14:50.803 --> 00:14:53.556
And so we need you
to demand more from others.

00:14:53.556 --> 00:14:55.349
We need people to really invest.

00:14:55.724 --> 00:14:58.185
I'm talking about the sports companies,

00:14:58.185 --> 00:15:01.105
the medical institutions
and other foundations.

00:15:01.647 --> 00:15:03.983
We don't just want lip service.

00:15:05.025 --> 00:15:07.987
There are athletes who are afraid
to tell their sponsors

00:15:08.028 --> 00:15:09.488
that they want to get pregnant

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:11.574
because they're afraid
they'll get dropped.

00:15:11.574 --> 00:15:14.702
There are athletes who don't want
to speak up when they're injured

00:15:14.702 --> 00:15:17.288
because they're afraid
they'll lose their sponsorship.

00:15:17.288 --> 00:15:19.373
It's not good enough
to just hang a shingle,

00:15:19.373 --> 00:15:21.167
make a pretty sports bra,

00:15:21.208 --> 00:15:22.543
or make a new sneaker.

00:15:22.585 --> 00:15:25.004
We need more than that,
we need true investment.

00:15:26.172 --> 00:15:29.925
We need investment in our girls
to let them know that they matter,

00:15:29.967 --> 00:15:32.887
particularly in this political climate.

00:15:32.887 --> 00:15:35.306
They need to hear it loud and clear.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:38.434
Now I love the men in my family.

00:15:38.434 --> 00:15:40.978
I have a father. I have a brother.

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:43.147
I have a husband and a son.

00:15:43.647 --> 00:15:46.525
But this world was built for them.

00:15:47.943 --> 00:15:51.155
I owe it to my grandmothers and my mother

00:15:51.197 --> 00:15:53.908
and my teammates and my daughters.

00:15:54.408 --> 00:15:56.368
And those women 50 years ago

00:15:56.410 --> 00:15:58.954
who fought so hard for Title IX,

00:15:58.996 --> 00:16:01.916
to keep fighting
and keep the mission going.

00:16:02.833 --> 00:16:05.085
So my challenge to you

00:16:05.127 --> 00:16:07.338
is that I hope that you'll join me

00:16:07.338 --> 00:16:09.507
and challenge the way things are.

00:16:09.548 --> 00:16:12.051
To encourage the investment in women.

00:16:12.051 --> 00:16:14.345
And please do not be afraid

00:16:14.386 --> 00:16:17.264
of seeing girls realize
their full potential.

00:16:17.765 --> 00:16:18.974
Thank you.

00:16:18.974 --> 00:16:21.936
(Applause)

